1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,588
Vendors publish a table, one model beats another by a few points on a named

2
00:00:04,588 --> 00:00:09,335
benchmark, and the natural reading is "this model is better." Read it that way often

3
00:00:09,335 --> 00:00:12,048
enough and you'll eventually ship the wrong one.

4
00:00:12,048 --> 00:00:16,682
A benchmark score is a real measurement of a real thing: how a model performs on a

5
00:00:16,682 --> 00:00:19,620
specific, fixed set of questions someone else wrote.

6
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:24,198
It is not automatically a measurement of how it'll perform on your specific task,

7
00:00:24,198 --> 00:00:27,024
with your specific data, in your specific product.

8
00:00:27,024 --> 00:00:28,945
Sometimes those correlate closely.

9
00:00:28,945 --> 00:00:31,092
Sometimes they don't correlate at all.

10
00:00:31,092 --> 00:00:35,444
This lecture is the handful of questions that tell you which situation you're

11
00:00:35,444 --> 00:00:39,626
actually in, before you commit to a model on the strength of a percentage.

12
00:00:39,626 --> 00:00:44,260
It matters more than it sounds like it should, because model choice is usually one

13
00:00:44,260 --> 00:00:46,237
of the harder decisions to reverse.

14
00:00:46,237 --> 00:00:50,984
Swapping a vendor after launch means re-running your evals, re-checking your prompts

15
00:00:50,984 --> 00:00:55,675
still hold, and possibly explaining to a room why the number everyone signed off on

16
00:00:55,675 --> 00:00:57,483
wasn't the number that mattered.

17
00:00:57,483 --> 00:01:02,061
Five minutes of reading the fine print before that decision is a lot cheaper than

18
00:01:02,061 --> 00:01:04,999
the version of this conversation that happens after.

19
00:01:05,549 --> 00:01:10,249
Four lines you'll see on almost every model announcement, and four questions that

20
00:01:10,249 --> 00:01:13,266
turn each one from a headline into an actual answer.

21
00:01:13,266 --> 00:01:17,965
A percentage on a named benchmark is real, but it's a score on questions somebody

22
00:01:17,965 --> 00:01:18,603
else chose.

23
00:01:18,603 --> 00:01:23,302
If none of those resemble what your feature actually does, the number is true and

24
00:01:23,302 --> 00:01:24,347
almost irrelevant.

25
00:01:24,347 --> 00:01:28,872
"Beats a competitor" does a lot of work in one sentence: it matters enormously

26
00:01:28,872 --> 00:01:33,629
whether that comparison came from an independent lab or the vendor grading its own

27
00:01:33,629 --> 00:01:34,152
homework.

28
00:01:34,152 --> 00:01:38,619
Context window and knowledge cutoff are two you already have a head start on.

29
00:01:38,619 --> 00:01:43,144
A stated context window is a ceiling, not a promise, and quality can slip well

30
00:01:43,144 --> 00:01:44,246
before it fills up.

31
00:01:44,246 --> 00:01:47,611
A cutoff date only gets older from the day it's published.

32
00:01:47,611 --> 00:01:49,874
None of these four lines are dishonest.

33
00:01:49,874 --> 00:01:53,181
They're just narrower than the headline makes them sound.

34
00:01:53,181 --> 00:01:57,822
Notice the pattern: each claim is precisely true and narrower than it reads at a

35
00:01:57,822 --> 00:01:58,228
glance.

36
00:01:58,228 --> 00:02:03,102
That's what happens whenever a complicated system gets compressed into one line on a

37
00:02:03,102 --> 00:02:04,030
comparison page.

38
00:02:04,030 --> 00:02:06,177
Your job isn't to catch anyone lying.

39
00:02:06,177 --> 00:02:10,412
It's to expand the line back into the question it was actually answering.

40
00:02:10,962 --> 00:02:15,563
Here's the shape of a typical general-purpose benchmark, and it's worth actually

41
00:02:15,563 --> 00:02:16,253
seeing once.

42
00:02:16,253 --> 00:02:20,796
Most public benchmarks are built from maths problems, coding challenges, trivia

43
00:02:20,796 --> 00:02:25,339
questions, and logic puzzles, because those are easy to grade automatically and

44
00:02:25,339 --> 00:02:27,697
cheap to scale to thousands of questions.

45
00:02:27,697 --> 00:02:30,284
That's a reasonable way to build a benchmark.

46
00:02:30,284 --> 00:02:34,080
It's just not a survey of everything a model might be asked to do.

47
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:38,738
If your feature summarises support tickets, or drafts marketing copy, or extracts

48
00:02:38,738 --> 00:02:41,441
fields from invoices, look again at that chart.

49
00:02:41,441 --> 00:02:45,524
There's usually a sliver, if anything, that resembles your actual task.

50
00:02:45,524 --> 00:02:50,182
A model can be genuinely excellent at maths and code and still be mediocre at the

51
00:02:50,182 --> 00:02:54,438
one thing you're about to ship, because the benchmark never tested for it.

52
00:02:54,438 --> 00:02:58,348
There's a second problem worth naming, quietly, alongside the first.

53
00:02:58,348 --> 00:03:00,936
Public benchmarks are, by definition, public.

54
00:03:00,936 --> 00:03:05,479
Their questions and answers sit somewhere on the internet, and internet text is

55
00:03:05,479 --> 00:03:07,492
exactly what these models train on.

56
00:03:07,492 --> 00:03:12,092
A model can score well partly because it's genuinely capable, and partly because

57
00:03:12,092 --> 00:03:14,680
it's seen something close to the test before.

58
00:03:14,680 --> 00:03:19,338
You can't easily tell which from the outside, which is one more reason a headline

59
00:03:19,338 --> 00:03:21,926
percentage deserves less weight than it gets.

60
00:03:22,476 --> 00:03:27,235
Say the honest caveat, because the lesson here isn't "ignore benchmarks." They're a

61
00:03:27,235 --> 00:03:28,038
genuine floor.

62
00:03:28,038 --> 00:03:32,740
A model scoring poorly across the board is telling you something real and probably

63
00:03:32,740 --> 00:03:33,542
worth heeding.

64
00:03:33,542 --> 00:03:38,359
What they can't do is promise a ceiling on your specific task, because your task, in

65
00:03:38,359 --> 00:03:41,742
almost every case, was never in the test set to begin with.

66
00:03:41,742 --> 00:03:46,272
That's exactly the gap your own golden set from Section four is built to close.

67
00:03:46,272 --> 00:03:49,368
The benchmark tells you a model probably isn't broken.

68
00:03:49,368 --> 00:03:53,783
Your fifty test cases tell you whether it's actually good at the thing you're

69
00:03:53,783 --> 00:03:54,299
shipping.

70
00:03:54,849 --> 00:03:59,317
That's the skill in one sentence: read what's actually being measured before you

71
00:03:59,317 --> 00:04:01,048
compare the numbers, not after.

72
00:04:01,048 --> 00:04:04,622
The exercise takes ten minutes and is worth doing once for real.

73
00:04:04,622 --> 00:04:08,755
Open the model card your team already relies on, and find those two things

74
00:04:08,755 --> 00:04:09,481
specifically.

75
00:04:09,481 --> 00:04:13,167
A card that's thorough about both is a good sign about the vendor.

76
00:04:13,167 --> 00:04:16,183
A card that's vague about both is worth noticing, too.

77
00:04:16,733 --> 00:04:18,490
One thing before you move on.

78
00:04:18,490 --> 00:04:23,339
Find one benchmark claim that influenced a real decision on your team, and check

79
00:04:23,339 --> 00:04:24,915
what it actually measured.

80
00:04:24,915 --> 00:04:26,794
Independent or vendor-reported?

81
00:04:26,794 --> 00:04:29,582
Recent, or from a superseded model generation?

82
00:04:29,582 --> 00:04:33,764
And above all, was anything resembling your actual task in it at all?

83
00:04:33,764 --> 00:04:38,309
If you can't answer that, neither could whoever made the original decision.

84
00:04:38,309 --> 00:04:41,461
That closes out the vocabulary half of this section.

85
00:04:41,461 --> 00:04:46,491
Next lecture is the payoff: twenty specific phrases that make an engineer trust you

86
00:04:46,491 --> 00:04:50,007
in the first five minutes of a conversation, not the last.
